findings.message:
Inconsistent Initialization Identified.

```
VULNERABILITY NAME: Inconsistent Initialization in TokenHandler.setToken
'''
DETAILED DESCRIPTION:
In `TokenHandler.setToken` (used to list a new token), when setting a cToken the function performs the following:
```
if (tokenStorage.tokenType == TokenType.cToken) {
    Token memory underlyingToken = getToken(currencyId, true);
    ERC20(underlyingToken.tokenAddress).approve(
        tokenStorage.tokenAddress,
        type(uint256).max
    );
}
```
The function assumes that the underlying token for the given `currencyId` has already been initialized. However, it never checks that `underlyingToken.tokenAddress` is non-zero. If governance calls `setToken` to set a cToken before the corresponding underlying token has been set, `getToken(currencyId, true)` returns a `Token` struct with `tokenAddress == address(0)`. The subsequent `approve` call is then made to `address(0)`. In Solidity 0.7.6, calling a function on an address with no code succeeds silently and returns empty data, so the `approve` does nothing and no allowance is set. The cToken is still stored in the token storage.

Later, when the system attempts to mint cTokens via `TokenHandler.mint`, the cToken contract will try to `transferFrom` the underlying tokens from the Notional contract. Since the approval was never set (and will never be set, even if the underlying token is configured afterwards), every mint operation will revert. This leaves the system in a broken, unusable state for that currency.

This is an inconsistent initialization vulnerability: the initialization of the cToken (which includes setting the required allowance) depends on the prior initialization of the underlying token. The code does not enforce this order, leading to unpredictable and unintended behavior.

RECOMMENDED MITIGATION/FIX (no upgrade code):
Add an explicit check to ensure the underlying token is already initialized before setting a cToken. For example:

```
if (tokenStorage.tokenType == TokenType.cToken) {
    Token memory underlyingToken = getToken(currencyId, true);
    require(underlyingToken.tokenAddress != address(0), "Underlying token not set");
    ERC20(underlyingToken.tokenAddress).approve(
        tokenStorage.tokenAddress,
        type(uint256).max
    );
}
```

Alternatively, restructure the initialization so that if the cToken is set first, the approval is set when the underlying token is later configured (e.g., in the underlying token branch, check if a cToken already exists and approve it). The simplest and safest fix is to require that the underlying token is initialized first, as shown above.
'''
```
Detector: InconsistentInitializationDetector
Classification: logic (no standard SWC)
